// Pull request author: swachchhanda000
// Change: update to existing rule
// Title: Potential AMSI COM Server Hijacking
// Sigma ID: 160d2780-31f7-4922-8b3a-efce30e63e96
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2023-01-04 | Modified: 2026-11-12
// ATT&CK: defense-impairment, T1685
// Description: Detects changes to the AMSI come server registry key in order disable AMSI scanning
//   functionalities. When AMSI attempts to starts its COM component, it will query its registered
//   CLSID and return a non-existent COM server. This causes a load failure and prevents any scanning
//   methods from being accessed, ultimately rendering AMSI useless
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://enigma0x3.net/2017/07/19/bypassing-amsi-via-com-server-hijacking/
//   - https://github.com/r00t-3xp10it/hacking-material-books/blob/43cb1e1932c16ff1f58b755bc9ab6b096046853f/obfuscation/simple_obfuscation.md#amsi-comreg-bypass
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/23e9c73742bad8c640fa459e2731b003b6f37175/rules/windows/registry/registry_set/registry_set_amsi_com_hijack.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where RegistryKey endswith "\\CLSID\\{fdb00e52-a214-4aa1-8fba-4357bb0072ec}\\InProcServer32\\(Default)" and (not((RegistryValueData in~ ("%%SystemRoot%%\\System32\\amsi.dll", "%%SystemRoot%%\\SysWOW64\\amsi.dll", "%%windir%%\\System32\\amsi.dll", "%%windir%%\\SysWOW64\\amsi.dll", "%SystemRoot%\\System32\\amsi.dll", "%SystemRoot%\\SysWOW64\\amsi.dll", "%windir%\\System32\\amsi.dll", "%windir%\\SysWOW64\\amsi.dll", "C:\\Windows\\System32\\amsi.dll", "C:\\Windows\\SysWOW64\\amsi.dll"))))
